hydrostatic-paradox-answer-why = The pressure at some depth in a liquid comes from the weight of the column above that place, p = ρ·g·h, — and it is the same at the same depth however wide or narrow the vessel is higher up. At the bottom the depth is alike — so the pressure is alike too, and the force on an equal bottom. Where, then, does the rest of the water’s weight go in the wide vessel? The sloping walls hold it: the water presses on them, and they on the stand. In the narrow one it is the other way: the sloping walls themselves press the water down, so the bottom gets more than the water weighs.
hydrostatic-paradox-answer-say = The manometer showed the same each time — ≈ { $F } on the bottom, though the water differed!
hydrostatic-paradox-answer-say-2 = Let us look at the manometer.
hydrostatic-paradox-answer-text = {" "}is alike
hydrostatic-paradox-answer-text-2 = {" "}in all three
hydrostatic-paradox-answer-text-3 = The bottom is { $value } across, its area S ≈ { $S } cm². { $l }. The pressure at the bottom p = ρ·g·Δh{ $value2 }, and the force on the bottom F = p·S ≈ { $F }{ $value3 }. Yet the water poured differed several times: in the widening vessel the water weighs more than it presses on the bottom, in the narrowing one less. The pressure on the bottom depends on the water’s height and the bottom’s area, not on the vessel’s shape or on how much water was poured — that is the hydrostatic paradox.
